The lexer rule `[\s]+` appears before `[
]+`, and since `\s` includes `
`, JISON's lexer will always consume newlines as SPACELIST tokens — the NL token is never emitted. This has a concrete correctness consequence: whitespace between two nodes is tokenized as a single SPACELIST including the newline(s) and any indentation spaces, so its `.length` reflects the newline count as well as indent spaces. A blank line between two same-depth siblings inflates the level of the second item, causing it to be incorrectly nested as a child of the first rather than remaining a sibling. Fix: move the `[
]+` rule above `[\s]+`, and update `[\s]` to not match newlines (e.g., use `[ 	]+` for SPACELIST) — the same pattern other mermaid parsers like `timeline.jison` follow.
